messages.content:
## Direct fix

Your `includeFiles` regex is **too strict for dev**. It requires the literal `@woven-canvas/vue/style.css` path, but in dev Vite resolves the file to whatever the package's `exports` map points at — realistically `node_modules/@woven-canvas/vue/dist/style.css` (and often with a `?v=<hash>` query, or served via `/@fs/…`). A single `dist/` segment between `vue/` and `style.css` makes the regex fail → PostCSS never sees the file → **no prefix is applied in dev** (in build the path happens to line up, or not — either way, this class of bug is path-shape dependent, which is exactly what you're seeing).

Fix: match the **package**, not one exact filename.

## 1. `vite.config.ts` — broaden the match

```js vite.config.ts
import { fileURLToPath, URL } from 'node:url'

import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
import vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue'
import vueJsx from '@vitejs/plugin-vue-jsx'
import pugPlugin from 'vite-plugin-pug'
import prefixer from 'postcss-prefix-selector'

import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite'

const {
  DEBUG,
} = process.env

const options = { pretty: true } // FIXME: pug pretty is deprecated!
const locals = { }

// https://vitejs.dev/config/
export default defineConfig({
  server: {
    allowedHosts: true,
    host: '0.0.0.0',
    watch: DEBUG ? { ignored: ["**/.codx/**"] } : null
  },
  plugins: [
    tailwindcss(),
    vue(),
    vueJsx(),
    pugPlugin(options, locals),
  ],
  resolve: {
    alias: {
      '@': fileURLToPath(new URL('./src', import.meta.url))
    }
  },
  css: {
    postcss: {
      plugins: [
        // Scope ONLY the woven-canvas stylesheet so its ".menu" (and other
        // generic classes) can't leak globally and break DaisyUI components.
        //
        // Match the WHOLE package, not one exact filename: in dev Vite resolves
        // the file to e.g. `@woven-canvas/vue/dist/style.css` (and may append a
        // `?v=<hash>` query or serve it via `/@fs/`). The previous pattern only
        // matched `@woven-canvas/vue/style.css`, so it silently never fired in dev.
        prefixer({
          includeFiles: [/@woven-canvas[\\/]vue[\\/]/],
          prefix: '.woven-canvas-scope',
          transform(prefix, selector, prefixedSelector) {
            // Never rewrite document-level or keyframe selectors
            if (/^(html|body|\*|:root)$/.test(selector)) return selector
            if (/^(from|to|\d+%|0%)$/.test(selector)) return selector
            return prefixedSelector
          }
        })
      ]
    }
  },
  optimizeDeps: {
    esbuildOptions: {
      target: 'esnext'
    }
  },
  build: {
    outDir: './dist',
    minify: false,
    emptyOutDir: true, // also necessary
    target: "esnext"
  }
})
```

Only one character-class change matters (`[\\/]` still normalizes Windows separators, so nothing else needs to change).

## 2. `src/assets/base.css` — leave clean (no change)

```css src/assets/base.css
@import "tailwindcss";
@plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";
@plugin "daisyui" {
  themes: all;
}
```

## 3. `src/components/canva/CanvaEditor.vue` — make sure the stylesheet is in the app graph

The package's JS entry *may* pull its own CSS, but relying on that is fragile. Pin it explicitly in `<script setup>` (imports only). Vite dedupes, so a double import is harmless.

```vue src/components/canva/CanvaEditor.vue
<script setup>
import '@woven-canvas/vue/style.css'
import { WovenCanvas } from '@woven-canvas/vue'
import {
  Block,
  Shape,
  Color,
  Text,
  Synced,
  UploadState,
  addComponent,
  getBlockDef,
  hasComponent,
  removeEntity,
  defineQuery,
  createEntity,
  RankBounds
} from '@woven-canvas/core'
</script>
```

> Keep exactly **one** entry point, and it must be a JS side-effect import. Never put `@import '@woven-canvas/vue/style.css'` back into `base.css` — Tailwind v4 inlines `@import` before PostCSS runs, so the prefixer would see `from = src/assets/base.css` and skip it.

## Verify in 5 seconds (dev)

Open DevTools → Elements → find the `<style>` tag that Vite injected for the woven stylesheet:

- **Good:** rules read `.woven-canvas-scope .menu { … }`
- **Bad:** rules read `.menu { … }` → still unprefixed; apply the fallback below.

You can also log the `from` to confirm the exact path:

```js
includeFiles: [/@woven-canvas[\\/]vue[\\/]/],
```

temporarily add `console.log('POSTCSS from =', this?.opts?.from)` inside a `postcss.plugins` wrapper — or simply grep the dev-served CSS URL in the Network tab; it will show you the real path shape.

## Fallback — if it *still* leaks in dev only

That means the CSS is being injected by the **dependency optimizer** (`node_modules/.vite/deps`) and never enters the project's PostCSS pipeline at all. Break the pre-bundle for that package only:

```js vite.config.ts
optimizeDeps: {
  exclude: ['@woven-canvas/vue'],
  esbuildOptions: {
    target: 'esnext'
  }
}
```

Then delete `node_modules/.vite` and restart. This only works if `@woven-canvas/vue` ships usable ESM (almost all modern Vue libs do) — if the dev server errors on the package, revert this and keep just the regex fix, which handles the common case.

## Why the previous attempt failed

| Concern | Previous | Now |
|--------|----------|-----|
| Match string | `@woven-canvas/vue/style.css` (exact) | `@woven-canvas/vue/…` (any file in the package) |
| Dev-resolved path (`…/vue/dist/style.css`) | ❌ no match → no prefix | ✅ match → prefixed |
| Query / `/@fs/` variants | ❌ fragile | ✅ robust |
| CSS source | relied on package's own import | explicit JS import in `CanvaEditor.vue` |

